from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "8. James 5:13 to 20 in Its Setting"),
("p", "Claim: James 5:13 to 20 is the only passage in the Scriptures that tells a sick person what to do about healing, and it is written to “the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad” (James 1:1). It is Israel’s passage. The anointing, the elders and the promised raising are read in the setting the letter itself fixes: the twelve tribes, their assembly, and the coming of the Lord that the letter says draws nigh. The Body prays for the sick because Paul commands prayer, not because James promises healing."),
("sub", "8.1 To whom James writes"),
("p", "The letter opens, “James, a servant of God and of the Lord Jesus Christ, to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad, greeting” (James 1:1). The address names the twelve tribes and says that they are scattered abroad. The writer calls them “my brethren” (James 1:2; 2:1), and he sets their meeting in a word the KJV renders “assembly.” “For if there come unto your assembly a man with a gold ring, in goodly apparel, and there come in also a poor man in vile raiment” (James 2:2). The word he uses there is the word for the gathering of a synagogue, and the volume reports it with the KJV’s “assembly.” The letter does not address the church of Corinth, the saints at Ephesus or the churches of Galatia, and it names none of the places Paul’s letters name."),
("sub", "8.2 The setting of the passage"),
("p", "The passage closes a letter that has been speaking of the last days and the coming of the Lord. The rich are told, “Go to now, ye rich men, weep and howl for your miseries that shall come upon you,” and are told that they have “heaped treasure together for the last days” (James 5:1, 3). The brethren are told, “Be patient therefore, brethren, unto the coming of the Lord,” and “Be ye also patient; stablish your hearts: for the coming of the Lord draweth nigh” (James 5:7, 8). They are given the prophets and Job as examples of patience: “Behold, we count them happy which endure. Ye have heard of the patience of Job, and have seen the end of the Lord; that the Lord is very pitiful, and of tender mercy” (James 5:11). The passage on the sick follows at once."),
("sub", "8.3 Verses 13 to 15"),
("p", "“Is any among you afflicted? let him pray. Is any merry? let him sing psalms. Is any sick among you? let him call for the elders of the church; and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord: And the prayer of faith shall save the sick, and the Lord shall raise him up; and if he have committed sins, they shall be forgiven him” (James 5:13–15). The passage gives three situations and three responses. The afflicted man prays, the merry man sings psalms and the sick man calls for the elders. The elders pray over him and anoint him with oil “in the name of the Lord.” The passage then states three results: the prayer of faith shall save the sick, the Lord shall raise him up, and if he has committed sins they shall be forgiven him."),
("p", "The passage is a command and a promise. It commands the sick person to call, and the elders to pray and anoint. It promises that the prayer of faith shall save the sick and that the Lord shall raise him up. The word “church” in the passage is the word for the assembly of the believers to whom the letter is written, and the elders are those of that assembly. The Twelve anointed the sick with oil in the cities of Israel (Mark 6:13), and the oil of James 5:14 is the same act in the same setting."),
("sub", "8.4 Verses 16 to 18"),
("p", "“Confess your faults one to another, and pray one for another, that ye may be healed. The effectual fervent prayer of a righteous man availeth much” (James 5:16). The verse joins confession, prayer and healing. The example given is Elijah: “Elias was a man subject to like passions as we are, and he prayed earnestly that it might not rain: and it rained not on the earth by the space of three years and six months. And he prayed again, and the heaven gave rain, and the earth brought forth her fruit” (James 5:17, 18). The example is from Israel’s history, a prophet of the land who prayed and whose prayer was answered by the LORD. The passage is of one piece with Israel’s Scriptures, which the chapter has already read in chapter 3: a people who hearken, a prophet who prays, a healing that comes."),
("sub", "8.5 Verses 19 and 20"),
("p", "The letter ends with a line on the erring: “Brethren, if any of you do err from the truth, and one convert him; Let him know, that he which converteth the sinner from the error of his way shall save a soul from death, and shall hide a multitude of sins” (James 5:19, 20). The last verses return to sins and to saving from death. The healing passage stands between the patience of the coming of the Lord and the turning of an erring brother. The whole is addressed to the twelve tribes."),
("sub", "8.6 Why the passage is not carried to the Body"),
("p", "The ruling is that James 5 is Israel’s passage. The anointing, the elders and the promised raising are Kingdom signs, and they are not norms of the Mystery. Three things in the text support the ruling. The address is to the twelve tribes (James 1:1). The meeting is an assembly of that people (James 2:2). And the letter looks to the coming of the Lord as near for the people it addresses (James 5:7, 8). The oil is the oil of Mark 6:13, which the Twelve used in Israel, and the elders’ prayer is set in the same setting as the commission of Matthew 10."),
("p", "The ruling does not say that the Body may not pray for the sick. It says that the Body’s ground for praying is another. Paul commands prayer for everything: “Be careful for nothing; but in every thing by prayer and supplication with thanksgiving let your requests be made known unto God” (Philippians 4:6). And he himself prayed about his thorn: “For this thing I besought the Lord thrice, that it might depart from me” (2 Corinthians 12:8). The Body prays for the sick because Paul commands prayer, and not because James promises healing. The two grounds produce prayer in both settings. They do not produce the same promise."),
("p", "The volume does not argue that James 5:14 to 16 is false or that it has no light for any reader. It argues that the passage is addressed to Israel, and that the promise it contains, that the Lord shall raise the sick man up, belongs to the setting in which it was given."),
("tbl", "James 5:13 to 20 in its setting.", [
    ("James 1:1", T("Jas 1:1"), "The letter is to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad."),
    ("James 5:7, 8", T("Jas 5:7, 8"), "The coming of the Lord draws nigh for the brethren addressed."),
    ("James 5:14, 15", T("Jas 5:14, 15"), "The sick call the elders; they pray and anoint; the prayer of faith shall save the sick."),
    ("James 5:17", T("Jas 5:17"), "The example is Elijah, a prophet of Israel."),
    ("Philippians 4:6", T("Phil 4:6"), "Paul commands prayer in everything for the Body."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "James is written to the twelve tribes scattered abroad, and James 5 is Israel’s passage.",
    "The anointing with oil is the act the Twelve performed in Israel, and the elders are those of the assembly addressed.",
    "The promise that the prayer of faith shall save the sick and the Lord shall raise him up belongs to the setting in which it was given.",
    "The Body prays for the sick because Paul commands prayer, not because James promises healing.",
  ]),
]
